  m_Name: DiaphragmCompressorData
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  engineName: Diaphragm Compressor
  engineDescription: A Diaphragm Compressor is a positive-displacement compressor
    that compresses gases using the reciprocating movement of a flexible diaphragm,
    preventing direct contact between the gas and the mechanical drive components.
    Its main components include the diaphragm, compression chamber, hydraulic or
    mechanical drive mechanism, suction valve, discharge valve, cylinder head, crankshaft,
    and cooling system. During operation, the diaphragm moves back and forth inside
    the compression chamber. During the suction stroke, the diaphragm moves away
    from the chamber, increasing its volume and drawing gas through the inlet valve.
    During the compression stroke, the diaphragm moves toward the chamber, reducing
    the gas volume and increasing its pressure. The compressed gas is then discharged
    through the outlet valve. Diaphragm compressors are widely used for high-purity
    gases, chemical processing, hydrogen systems, laboratories, pharmaceutical applications,
    and gas handling where contamination must be minimized. From an engineering perspective,
    understanding diaphragm stresses, compression ratio, gas purity, sealing, pressure
    pulsation, heat transfer, fatigue, cooling, and maintenance is essential for
    safe and reliable operation.
  engineCategory: Engineering
